  "text_md": "PERQUIRE. To search into. Clobery's Divine Glimpses, 1659, p. 73. PERR. (1) Perry. (2) A pearl.\n\nGl. u. 38 ; Wilbraham, p. 107 ; Forby, ii. 249. To perk one's self up, to adorn. To perk up again, to recover from sickness. A perch. \"Ov-^t the perke to\n\n(2) A pearl.\n\nPERRE. A dish in oỈd cookery, made chiefy of peas, onions, and spices:\n\n(4) A perch. Suffolk. the to 124. It also occurs in Ovyr perke\n\nPERRIER. A kind of short mortar, formerly much used for stone shot.\n\nReliq. Antiq. i. 294. (5) A wooden frame against which sawn timber is set up to dry. East. PERKERS. Young rooks. North.\n\nPERRIWINKLE_ A periwig: Stubbe.\n\nPERRONENDERE. pardoner. Hearne.\n\nPERKERS. Young rooks. North.\n\nPERRY-DANCERS. The aurora borealis.   East. PERRYE. (1) A squall.\n\nPERKIN. Water cyder.\n\nPERKY Saucy obstinate.\n\nIt happened Harold his sonne to arrive at Pountlou against his will, by occaslon of sudden perry, Or contrarle winde, that arose whlle he was on seaboorde.\n\nPERLATANE.\n\nThe hauUe also of this palace was sett fulle of ymagez of golde, and bitwix thame stode pei-latanea of golde, in the branches of whilke ther were many maners of fewles. MS. Lincoln A. i. 17, f. 25.\n\n(2)A little cur North. dog;\n\n(3) Precious stones\n\njewels. (A-N) And alle was set wlth perrve, Ther was never no better Ip Crystyante.\n\nPERLESY. pleurisy.\n\nwere with a perlesy, that alle his lymes dryes, that he may na gud do als he sulde. MS. Lincoln A. i. 17. f. 246.\n\nPERS. (1) Persia. We woot bothe\n\nPERLID. Ornamented with pearls ; studded with any ornaments. And many a perZtdgarnement\n\nWe woot bothe bl story and vers That the kyndam of Grace and Pars\n\nAnd many 8 perlỉd garncment Embroudid day.\n\nWere hede kyngus In forme tlde.\n\nCursor Mundi, MS: Coll. Trin. Cantab. f. 132 (2) Company.\n\n134, f. 54. PERLIN. The piece of timber which runs along under the middle part of the spars or bearers of a roof, to give such bearers additional strength. PERLOWES. PerUous. PaUgrave.\n\nAl we wite it thl defaut,\n\nArthour and Merlin, p. 9. (3) Sky, or blueish gray colour. There was a kind of cloth so called. PERSAUNT. {A.-N.)\n\nPERLOWES. Perilous. Palsgrave:\n\nPERSAUNI. Piercing(A-N)\n\nPERMAFAY. By my faith. (A-N:)\n\nPERMANSIE: Mlagic necromancy-\n\nThat of the stremis every maner wyzte Astonied was, weren bryzte and shene, Ant to the ye for peraaunt for to sene. they\n\nPERN. (1) To prosper: Somerset .\n\n(2) To pick and dress birds, particularly applied to dressing the heron.\n\nFor thy perseynt charit^. Gowei; MS. Soc. Antiq.\n\nPERNASO. Mount Parnassus:\n\nPERNEL. The pimpernel, a fower that always shuts its blossoms before rain. up\n\n134, f. 109. PERSCRUTE. To search through. {Lat.) Used by Andrew Borde, Brit. Bibl. iv. 24. PERSE. Equality. (A.-N.)\n\nBut these tender pernels must have one gown for the another for the night day,\n\nPERSE. Equality. (A-N) PERSEL. Parsley. PERSEVER. To persevere. Pegge.\n\nPilkington', Worke, P. 56. PERPEND. To consider attentively_\n\nShak.\n\nWhether a daw sit, whether a daw fy, Whether & daw stand, In what case soever 8 daw persever,\n\nYou'll quickly know, If you do well perpend,\n\nAnd observe rlghtly what's the proper end.\n\nSong  , 1661, p. 182.\n\nPERPENDICLE. The plumb drant. This word occurs in an old treatise on mensuration, in MS. Sloane213. PERPENTINE. A porcupine. \" Perpoynt, %*-\n\nTarlton' 1611. Jcet8,\n\nPERSIAN-WHEEL. An raise a quantity of water sufficient for overflowing lands, that border in the banks of rivers, where the streams lie so low, as to be incapable of doing it. PERSON. A mask, or actor. {Lat.)\n\ntrix\" Pr. Parv. The form perpenfine occurs in Shakespeare, most incorrectly altered to porcupine by modern editors. It is the genuine old word. PERPE NT-STONE. A large stone reaching\n\nPERSON. A mask, or actor. (Lat:)\n\nthrough a wall so as to appear on both sides of it. Oxf. Gl. Arch. p. 280. In the North of England, a thin wall, the stones of which are built on the edge, is called a perpent. PERPETUANA. A kind of glossy cloth, gene-\n\nPersonally visible.\n\nMy saied lorde of Winchester saied unto the kyng that the kyng his father, B0 vis{tcd   wIth sickenesse; was pot personable.\n\nHall , Henry VI.f.13. PERSONE. man. Generally, man of or rector of a church.\n\nPERPETUANA. A kind of glossy cloth, generally called everlasting.\n\ndignity, & parson PERSORE.\n\nA piercing-iron.\n\nPERPLANTED. Planted securely .\n\nThat at I sey it shall be sure Whi chyd je iche one with other ? Wote 56 wele I ame jour brother ! Therefore none contrary me. Fore as I sey so schall it be.\n\nRequlrynge theim as his especlall truste and confdence that   they would and mete hym by the vaye wIth all dlligent preparaclon.\n\nHall , Richurd IlI.f. 27.\n\nForc as\n\n",
